This work demonstrates experimentally, theoretically, and schematically how the effective periodicity of an effective periodic potential experienced by photons can be altered by arranging alternating magnetic inclusions with opposite spin orientations on an on-chip optical waveguide, creating a directional-sensitive magnetophotonic surface lattice. The phenomenon results in effective potentials that exhibit variable periodicity depending on the propagation direction relative to the inclusion arrangement, without such lattice effects being observable when spins are randomly oriented. Our finding enables the realization of photonic artificial materials with complex, tunable artificial crystal structures on a unified platform.

